      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 23,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p5_b8",
      "text": "Models and Datasets. Our experiments utilize the LLaVA-\nv1.5 (Liu et al., 2024b) (7B, 13B) and LLaVA-NEXT (3B)\nmodels. For LBR (VIT), we train all models on the official\nLLaVA-v1.5 instruction tuning dataset, a widely-recognized\ndataset for visual instruction tuning. For LBP (DPO), we\nuse the LLaVA-v1.5 models and train on RLHF-V (Yu et al.,\n2024a) (5.7K pairs), supplemented by 1K and 10K pairs\nfrom VLFeedback (Li et al., 2024) for scalability analysis.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.19,
        416.12,
        291.1,
        509.83
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        1664,
        1164,
        2039
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.92,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 457,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p5_b9",
      "text": "Evaluation Benchmarks. We evaluate LBR on a compre-\nhensive suite of benchmarks spanning four key categories:\nGeneral LVLM Benchmarks, Text-intensive Tasks, Visual\nQA Tasks, and Image Caption Tasks. For LBP, our eval-\nuation focuses on three hallucination-centric benchmarks:\nMMHalBench, AMBER (which comprises both Generative\nand Discriminative tasks), and Object HalBench. Further\nbenchmark details are in Appendix B.1.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        517.68,
        291.1,
        611.45
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2070,
        1164,
        2445
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.99,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 421,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p5_b10",
      "text": "Baselines. For LBR, we compare against the vanilla VIT\nbaseline. For LBP, We first compare LBP with DPO variants\nsuch as V-DPO (Xie et al., 2024) and MFPO (Jiang et al.,\n2024b), as they use the same model and data, allowing for\ndirect comparison. In additional experiments, we compare\nwith vanilla DPO (with Margin Loss), which shares the\nsame training process, data, and hyper-parameters as LBR,\nbut with a different learning objective. In addition, we\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.08,
        619.25,
        290.69,
        713.09
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2476,
        1162,
        2852
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 453,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p5_b11",
      "text": "also report results from several representative models and\nmethods for reference. Further details are in Appendix B.2.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        301.69,
        543.17,
        323.68
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1206,
        2172,
